    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1 data-split>Ten years in software, then I built my own&nbsp;thing</h1>
    <p class="lede prose" style="margin-top:18px">Both halves taught me the same thing: the hard part is working out what someone actually needs, and then answering for what you ship.</p>

    <div class="lead-grid" style="margin-top:30px">
      <div class="longform">
        <p class="rv">I have spent ten years building customer-facing software, five of them in finance, where a product has to satisfy a regulator before it can satisfy a user. Account opening, deposits, lending, device binding, biometrics, know-your-customer. Three of those five were at Taiwan's first digital-only bank, where I led nine engineers and the delivery of five vendors.</p>
        <p class="rv">Inside a bank the interesting problem is rarely the code. It is working out what a rule actually requires, what it costs the person on the other side when you get it wrong, and how to sequence a change so nothing is ever in a state it should not be in. That is the part of my work I am still best at.</p>
        <p class="rv">A bank also settles two things before you arrive. The regulator sets the problem, and what a banking app already looks like sets the shape of the answer. Nobody has to ask whether the thing should exist.</p>
        <p class="rv">So in late 2025 I started a company and gave myself the question a bank never has to ask: what should exist, and would anyone pay for it.</p>
        <p class="rv">Course Kit is the answer I got, built alongside a full-time role. I did the market research, the design, the app, the backend, the payments, the pricing, the brand and the marketing, because there was nobody to hand any of it to. AI tooling is what makes that scope possible for one person. Four years ago it would have taken a team. Every decision came back to me, including the ones I got wrong and had to pay for later.</p>
        <p class="rv">None of that was a move away from engineering. It was a way to become the kind of engineer who can see the whole line, from what a customer actually needs to what it costs to keep running. That is the line a Forward Deployed Engineer works along, and it is the job I want next.</p>

        <h2 class="rv">What I am building</h2>
        <p class="rv">Course Kit is a course-management app I designed, built and shipped alone on iOS, Android and a web storefront, with three payment rails in production and paying customers. It runs on Vibe Code Home, a platform a partner and I built so that people who can write code can also sell what they make.</p>

        <h2 class="rv">What the bank left me with</h2>
        <p class="rv">I am comfortable when the requirements belong to someone who is not an engineer and does not describe them in features. I am comfortable building against a specification I do not control and cannot change. And I am comfortable being the person who explains, to a room that does not write code, why something takes the shape it does. That last one used to be the part of the job I did on the side. It is now most of what I think the work is.</p>
      </div>
